nodecode-secure-audit is a rewrite of PensiveFei's PensiveFei/dsh-secure-audit:

  https://github.com/PensiveFei/dsh-secure-audit
  commit ae94c94183ef785702d34cc90e08705e8c32177b
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 dsh-secure-audit contributors (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the four tools of the original became three, in Common Lisp against
Nodecode's own seams. security_scan_text keeps the rule engine's shape -- a
severity-weighted decision with allow/review/block, per-rule reasons and
masked snippets, English and Chinese rows, and the normalisation layer that
folds zero-width and full-width characters, lookalike Cyrillic and Greek
letters and bounded base64 -- with the thresholds read from the cell's
section. security_redact_text keeps the mainland-China-first pattern table:
mobile numbers, ID cards validated by their check digit, bank cards validated
by Luhn, emails, IPv4 addresses, vendor API keys and credentials in a URL.
security_audit keeps the read-only posture and the one hard rule of the
original -- it names what it finds and never quotes a secret -- over the
checks that transfer to this host: config secrets, config file permissions,
secret-like environment variables, the installed cell inventory and a gateway
bound to every interface.

Left out, and why: security_redact_json (the fourth tool) handled JSON by key
name; Nodecode's tool boundary already hands the model a typed record, and a
text-in JSON-out variant would have needed a JSON parser for no new coverage.
The original's session-file PII scan, its /proc/net wildcard lookup, its OWASP
mapping and its multi-frame zstd session reader are specific to the harness it
shipped for; Nodecode stores no sessions in that shape. The opt-in live
registry advisory lookup and the pluggable Ollama classifier were dropped so
the cell stays offline and keyless. The skill markdown became the cell's own
manual topic, (help :secure-audit).
